{{- $sdkName := (sanitizeClassName .Global.AST.MainSDK.Type.Name) -}}
{{- if useOkHttp -}}
The Java SDK makes API calls through the default `SpeakeasyHTTPClient`, an `HTTPClient` implementation backed by
[OkHttp](https://square.github.io/okhttp/) that supports hooks for modifying requests and handling responses or errors.
Requests and responses use the `HttpRequest` and `HttpResponse` types from the `{{templatePackageName}}.utils.transport` package.
The SDK
{{- if asyncEnabled}} and its async counterpart implement `AutoCloseable`; closing either
{{- else}} implements `AutoCloseable`; closing it
{{- end}} releases the default client's dispatcher, connection pool, and optional cache.
Use try-with-resources or call `close()` after all requests and streams have completed.
{{- if springBootStarterEnabled}}
Do not however manually close a Spring-managed SDK because the Spring container owns and closes its `HTTPClient` bean.
{{- end}}

For custom transport behavior you may provide your own `HTTPClient` implementation. Custom implementations inherit a no-op
`close()` and should override it only when they own the resources they use. If a closeable custom client is shared by
multiple SDK instances, coordinate its lifetime externally or use a borrowing wrapper whose `close()` remains a no-op.
The following example wraps the default client to add a custom header, so it also delegates `close()` to that client:

```java
import {{templatePackageName}}.{{$sdkName}};
import {{templatePackageName}}.utils.HTTPClient;
import {{templatePackageName}}.utils.SpeakeasyHTTPClient;
import {{templatePackageName}}.utils.transport.HttpRequest;
import {{templatePackageName}}.utils.transport.HttpResponse;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStream;
{{- if asyncEnabled}}
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
{{- end}}

public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Create a custom HTTP client with hooks
        HTTPClient httpClient = new HTTPClient() {
            private final HTTPClient defaultClient = new SpeakeasyHTTPClient();

            @Override
            public HttpResponse<InputStream> send(HttpRequest request) throws IOException {
                // Add a custom header using HttpRequest.toBuilder()
                HttpRequest modifiedRequest = request.toBuilder()
                    .header("x-custom-header", "custom value")
                    .build();

                try {
                    HttpResponse<InputStream> response = defaultClient.send(modifiedRequest);
                    // Log successful response
                    System.out.println("Request successful: " + response.statusCode());
                    return response;
                } catch (Exception error) {
                    // Log error
                    System.err.println("Request failed: " + error.getMessage());
                    throw error;
                }
            }
{{- if asyncEnabled}}

            @Override
            public CompletableFuture<HttpResponse<InputStream>> sendAsync(HttpRequest request) {
                // Add a custom header using HttpRequest.toBuilder()
                HttpRequest modifiedRequest = request.toBuilder()
                    .header("x-custom-header", "custom value")
                    .build();
                return defaultClient.sendAsync(modifiedRequest);
            }
{{- end}}

            @Override
            public void close() throws Exception {
                // This custom HTTPClient owns defaultClient, so it is responsible for closing it.
                defaultClient.close();
            }
        };

        try ({{$sdkName}} sdk = {{$sdkName}}.builder()
                .client(httpClient)
                .build()) {

            // SDK calls here

            // sdk.close() runs upon exiting the try block
        }
    }
}
```
{{- if asyncEnabled}}

The `sendAsync` method powers the async SDK methods and returns a `CompletableFuture<HttpResponse<InputStream>>` that
completes as soon as the response headers arrive. Blocking body reading and parsing run on a follow-up asynchronous
completion stage rather than on the HTTP client's dispatcher threads.
{{- end}}

You can also enable debug logging on the default `SpeakeasyHTTPClient`:

```java
import {{templatePackageName}}.{{$sdkName}};
import {{templatePackageName}}.utils.SpeakeasyHTTPClient;

public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        SpeakeasyHTTPClient httpClient = new SpeakeasyHTTPClient();
        httpClient.enableDebugLogging(true);

        try ({{$sdkName}} sdk = {{$sdkName}}.builder()
                .client(httpClient)
                .build()) {

            // SDK calls here

            // sdk.close() runs upon exiting the try block
        }
    }
}
```
{{- else}}
The Java SDK makes API calls using an `HTTPClient` that wraps the native
[HttpClient](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/11/docs/api/java.net.http/java/net/http/HttpClient.html). This
client provides the ability to attach hooks around the request lifecycle that can be used to modify the request or handle
errors and response.

The `HTTPClient` interface allows you to either use the default `SpeakeasyHTTPClient` that comes with the SDK,
or provide your own custom implementation with customized configuration such as custom executors, SSL context,
connection pools, and other HTTP client settings.

The interface provides synchronous (`send`) methods{{if asyncEnabled}} and asynchronous (`sendAsync`) methods. The `sendAsync` method
is used to power the async SDK methods and returns a `CompletableFuture<HttpResponse<Blob>>` for non-blocking operations{{end}}.

The following example shows how to add a custom header and handle errors:

```java
import {{templatePackageName}}.{{$sdkName}};
import {{templatePackageName}}.utils.HTTPClient;
import {{templatePackageName}}.utils.SpeakeasyHTTPClient;
import {{templatePackageName}}.utils.Utils;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.URISyntaxException;
import java.net.http.HttpRequest;
import java.net.http.HttpResponse;
import java.io.InputStream;
import java.time.Duration;

public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a custom HTTP client with hooks
        HTTPClient httpClient = new HTTPClient() {
            private final HTTPClient defaultClient = new SpeakeasyHTTPClient();
            
            @Override
            public HttpResponse<InputStream> send(HttpRequest request) throws IOException, URISyntaxException, InterruptedException {
                // Add custom header and timeout using Utils.copy()
                HttpRequest modifiedRequest = Utils.copy(request)
                    .header("x-custom-header", "custom value")
                    .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
                    .build();
                    
                try {
                    HttpResponse<InputStream> response = defaultClient.send(modifiedRequest);
                    // Log successful response
                    System.out.println("Request successful: " + response.statusCode());
                    return response;
                } catch (Exception error) {
                    // Log error
                    System.err.println("Request failed: " + error.getMessage());
                    throw error;
                }
            }
        };

        {{$sdkName}} sdk = {{$sdkName}}.builder()
            .client(httpClient)
            .build();
    }
}
```

<details>
<summary>Custom HTTP Client Configuration</summary>

You can also provide a completely custom HTTP client with your own configuration:

```java
import {{templatePackageName}}.{{$sdkName}};
import {{templatePackageName}}.utils.HTTPClient;{{if asyncEnabled}}
import {{templatePackageName}}.utils.Blob;
import {{templatePackageName}}.utils.ResponseWithBody;{{end}}

import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.URISyntaxException;
import java.net.http.HttpClient;
import java.net.http.HttpRequest;
import java.net.http.HttpResponse;
import java.io.InputStream;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;{{if asyncEnabled}}
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;{{end}}

public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Custom HTTP client with custom configuration
        HTTPClient customHttpClient = new HTTPClient() {
            private final HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
                .executor(Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10))
                .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
                // .sslContext(customSslContext) // Add custom SSL context if needed
                .build();

            @Override
            public HttpResponse<InputStream> send(HttpRequest request) throws IOException, URISyntaxException, InterruptedException {
                return client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofInputStream());
            }
{{if asyncEnabled}}
            @Override
            public CompletableFuture<HttpResponse<Blob>> sendAsync(HttpRequest request) {
                // Convert response to HttpResponse<Blob> for async operations
                return client.sendAsync(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofPublisher())
                    .thenApply(resp -> new ResponseWithBody<>(resp, Blob::from));
            }
{{end}}        };

        {{$sdkName}} sdk = {{$sdkName}}.builder()
            .client(customHttpClient)
            .build();
    }
}
```

</details>

You can also enable debug logging on the default `SpeakeasyHTTPClient`:

```java
import {{templatePackageName}}.{{$sdkName}};
import {{templatePackageName}}.utils.SpeakeasyHTTPClient;

public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpeakeasyHTTPClient httpClient = new SpeakeasyHTTPClient();
        httpClient.enableDebugLogging(true);

        {{$sdkName}} sdk = {{$sdkName}}.builder()
            .client(httpClient)
            .build();
    }
}
```
{{- end}}